<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.8. Laplace's equation in rectangular coordinates</title>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>

  <definition xml:id="def-laplace-eqn">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <term>Laplace's equation</term> describes a system in a steady-state, or a state of 
        equilibrium, which does not depend on time.  It is given by
        <md>
          \nabla^2 u = 0,
        </md>
        where <m>\ds \nabla^2 u = \pdtwo{u}{x} + \pdtwo{u}{y}</m> for <m>u(x,y)</m> in two dimensions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Solutions of Laplace's equation are called <term>harmonic functions</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose we have a rectangular plate whose dimensions are
  <m>10\times5</m>. Then Laplace's equation
  describes the steady state temperature distribution <m>u(x,y)</m> in the
  plate (steady state of the heat equation here).  Solve Laplace's equation in the rectangle with the boundary
  conditions shown.  In particular, <m>u=1</m> on the top and <m>0</m> on the other sides.  
  <!--You should be able to draw the graph
  of the solution as well.-->
  </p>

<sidebyside width="20% 25%">
 
  <image width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
      <shortdescription>A rectangle with the bottom left corner at the origin extending 10 units right and 5 units up.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-17-pic1">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.5,12,7)">
            <axes  decorations="no" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(0,10,10)"
            vticks="(0,5,5)" hlabels="(0,10,10)" vlabels="(0,5,5)"/>
            <rectangle lower-left="(0,0)" dimensions="(10,5)"
               fill="gray" thickness="2" stroke="black" opacity="0.5" />
          <label anchor="(5,0)" alignment="s">
           <m>0</m>
          </label>
          <label anchor="(0,2.5)" alignment="w">
           <m>0</m>
          </label>
          <label anchor="(10,2.5)" alignment="e">
           <m>0</m>
          </label>
          <label anchor="(5,5)" alignment="n">
           <m>1</m>
          </label>
    
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    
    <p>
      <md>
      \begin{aligned}
        \nabla^2 u \amp  = 0 \\
        u(x,0) \amp  = 0 \\
        u(0,y) \amp  = 0 \\
        u(10,y) \amp  = 0 \\
        u(x,5) \amp  = 1
      \end{aligned}
      </md>
    </p>
     
</sidebyside>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that you have an infinite rectangular plate;  that is,
  the plate is between <m>0\leq x\leq \pi</m> and <m>0\leq y</m>.  Solve Laplace's
  equation in this plate with the boundary conditions given.
      </p>
  <sidebyside width="20% 25%">
 
  <image width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
      <shortdescription>An open rectangle with the bottom left corner at the origin extending pi units right and infinite units up.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-17-pic2">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.5,pi+1.5,8.5)">
            <axes  decorations="no" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(0,10,10)"
            hlabels="(0,10,10)" />
          <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(0,8)" arrows="1" thickness="5" />
          <line p1="(pi,0)" p2="(pi,8)" arrows="1" thickness="5" />
          <line p1="(0,0)" p2="(pi,0)" arrows="0" thickness="5" />
          <label anchor="(pi/2,0)" alignment="s">
           <m>1</m>
          </label>
          <label anchor="(0,2.5)" alignment="w">
           <m>0</m>
          </label>
          <label anchor="(pi,2.5)" alignment="e">
           <m>0</m>
          </label>
          <label anchor="(pi/2,7)" alignment="ne" offset="(1,14)">
           <m>o</m>
          </label>

          <path start="(pi/2,6)" closed="no" arrows="1" stroke="black" thickness="3">
      <rlineto point="(0,2)"
               decoration="wave; number=1; dimensions=(10,10)"/>
    </path>
    
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    
    <p>
      <md>
      \begin{aligned}
        u(x,0) \amp  = 1 \\
        u(0,y) \amp  = 0 \\
        u(\pi,y) \amp  = 0 \\
        \lim\limits_{y\rightarrow\infty} u(x,y) \amp  = 0
      \end{aligned}
      </md>
    </p>
</sidebyside>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>How would the solution to the first problem be different if we
  change the  
  boundary conditions?  (P.S. Can you identify what the boundary conditions mean physically?)</p>
      <p><md>
  \begin{aligned}
    \nabla^2 u \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x,0) \amp  = 0 \\
    \pd{u}{x}(0,y) \amp  = 0 \\
    \pd{u}{x}(10,y) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x,5) \amp  = 1
  \end{aligned}
  </md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
